  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The lead lasted eight innings and died in one. Brady Basso couldn't get out of the second, Jack Perkins held Kansas City scoreless through 3.2 IP, but the bullpen chain frayed when it mattered most. Hogan Harris gave up two runs in the ninth and nobody got another at-bat. This is what it looks like when depth fails in the third spot of a relief sequence.",
    "what_this_means": "They led 3-0 after the first inning and never trailed until the ninth. Ten runners reached in scoring position. They converted three of them into runs. Jeff McNeil went 2-for-4 with an RBI, Tommy White delivered one as well, and that was enough to hold until it wasn't. The roster had chances. Plural, real chances. But a one-run lead is not a margin. Harris's arm was the door that swung shut, and the Royals walked through it in the last frame. This is not a team losing because it couldn't score. This is a team losing because it couldn't survive the moment after it did.",
    "what_to_watch": "Kansas City again tonight at 4:40 PM PT. Same pitcher, same ballpark, same situation. A chance to correct the same mistake.",
